C. Q27 plus shell<=41 repair

To test whether the two Q27-feasible deletion sets contain any packet that is
hard for the next ordinary shells, rerun the MILP with obligations

  Q27 union {2,3,...,41}.

The only new plain shells not already in Q27 are

  29, 31, 33, 37, 39, 41.

Exact repair:

  deleted=(28,42,56,84), obligations=670:
    infeasible in 8.20s.

  deleted=(42,56,70,84), obligations=540:
    infeasible in 5.63s.

Therefore every primitive carry-window row retaining exactly eight core speeds
has either a Q27 witness or a plain-shell witness with q<=41.

D. Proof-shaped consequence

Combining this with HYP-2465:

  In the carry window 1..1092, every primitive replacement row retaining at
  least eight of the twelve core speeds has either a Q27 witness or a plain
  denominator witness q<=41.

Thus a normalized LRC14 row with no Q27 witness and no small plain q<=41 witness
must either:

  * delete at least five core speeds,
  * leave the carry window,
  * violate the replacement-normalization model, or
  * fall into a named owner/Bprime/positive-measure exception channel.

This replaces the original false universal "eight-core forces Q27" target with
the stronger Church-style finite-exception statement: the only eight-core Q27
packets found are not LRC threats; they open immediately at q=31 or q=33 and by
Bprime/positive-measure certificates.
